00:21:51.272 --> 00:21:54.052
shield thickness that you have, the more radiation you're blocking.

342
00:21:54.472 --> 00:21:58.232
However, at a certain point, it's not really

343
00:21:59.992 --> 00:22:00.652
beneficial.

344
00:22:02.132 --> 00:22:04.132
It's diminishing returns, essentially.

345
00:22:05.992 --> 00:22:09.222
Then you would want to use rad-hard processors,

346
00:22:09.852 --> 00:22:13.352
and there's a few different ways that processors can be considered

347
00:22:13.392 --> 00:22:17.152
rad-hard. One is through oxide insulation.

348
00:22:17.632 --> 00:22:21.572
So thin layers of oxide insulation are used to separate the active circuitry

349
00:22:21.612 --> 00:22:25.092
from the bulk silicon substrate. Then there's triple modular

350
00:22:25.152 --> 00:22:28.792
redundancy, or TMR, which uses three identical

351
00:22:28.872 --> 00:22:32.712
circuits and uses majority rules if there's any discrepancies between the

352
00:22:32.752 --> 00:22:36.632
circuits. So if you have one circuit that's malfunctioning due

353
00:22:36.692 --> 00:22:40.652
to a single event effect, then the

354
00:22:40.692 --> 00:22:44.212
other two most likely won't be, and so you can use those to

355
00:22:44.252 --> 00:22:46.222
determine which

356
00:22:47.832 --> 00:22:49.382
result is accurate.

357
00:22:50.962 --> 00:22:54.762
Then there's just the general idea of using lower voltages, which can

358
00:22:54.792 --> 00:22:57.252
reduce the risk of single event latch ups from occurring.

359
00:22:58.312 --> 00:23:01.732
Then there's this technique called error detection by duplicated

360
00:23:01.792 --> 00:23:02.552
instructions,

361
00:23:03.452 --> 00:23:06.992
where you, for critical instructions, duplicate original

362
00:23:07.012 --> 00:23:10.932
instructions with different registers and variables, and then once they're

363
00:23:10.952 --> 00:23:14.852
executed, if there's a mismatch detected, then the error handling software is

364
00:23:14.892 --> 00:23:18.452
invoked. Unlike the triple modular redundancy, where you can resolve the error

365
00:23:18.492 --> 00:23:22.432
right there pretty much with just majority rules, the error

366
00:23:22.472 --> 00:23:23.632
correction has to step in.

367
00:23:25.432 --> 00:23:28.872
Then there are error correction codes, ECC,

368
00:23:30.132 --> 00:23:33.872
which are basically codes

369
00:23:33.932 --> 00:23:37.372
that are added to data to verify that there have been no changes.

370
00:23:37.382 --> 00:23:41.282
So one example of this is EDAC, which is error detection and control, which

371
00:23:41.372 --> 00:23:45.052
adds redundant bits to data, allowing the computer to detect if an error has

372
00:23:45.072 --> 00:23:48.972
occurred by referencing the metadata provided by the redundant bits.

373
00:23:50.392 --> 00:23:53.612
Then there is something called hardware scrubbing, where essentially,

374
00:23:54.332 --> 00:23:56.252
during CPU downtime,

375
00:23:57.792 --> 00:24:01.512
it runs a system that detects radiation-induced errors

376
00:24:01.632 --> 00:24:04.412
and rewrites the data, if it's able to

377
00:24:06.372 --> 00:24:07.332
Then finally,

378
00:24:08.832 --> 00:24:12.632
just like how if you're having an issue with your computer and usually tech will

379
00:24:12.672 --> 00:24:13.272
tell you, "Hey,

380
00:24:14.192 --> 00:24:15.712
have you tried turning it on and off again?"

381
00:24:16.652 --> 00:24:19.892
This works really well for clearing temporary errors

382
00:24:20.032 --> 00:24:22.802
created by radiation,

383
00:24:23.892 --> 00:24:27.262
because it clears the memory and then it forces the hardware to reinitialize,

384
00:24:27.752 --> 00:24:29.812
helping to restore it back to a functioning state.

